<Layout 
  title="How to Calculate Bounce Back Loan Repayments (2026 Guide)" 
  description="Learn exactly how Bounce Back Loan repayments are calculated, how the 2.5% interest rate works, and how to navigate the Pay As You Grow (PAYG) options in 2026."
>
  <main class="container">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>How to Calculate Bounce Back Loan Repayments</h1>
      <p class="meta">Written by the Engineering Team at RevoCalc | Updated for 2026</p>

      <p>The Bounce Back Loan Scheme (BBLS) was a lifeline for hundreds of thousands of UK businesses during the COVID-19 pandemic. The government guaranteed 100% of the loans, allowing small businesses to borrow between £2,000 and £50,000 with no upfront fees.</p>

      <p>If you took out a Bounce Back Loan at the peak of the scheme in 2020 or 2021, you are now well into the repayment phase. For many businesses, the original 6-year term is ending in 2026 or 2027. Understanding exactly how your monthly payments are calculated—and what happens if you still can't afford them—is critical.</p>

      <h2>The Math: How Bounce Back Loan Interest Works</h2>
      <p>Unlike a standard commercial mortgage or loan, the Bounce Back Loan does not use a traditional amortization formula where interest is front-loaded. Instead, it uses a <strong>flat interest rate</strong>.</p>
      
      <p>The annual interest rate is fixed at <strong>2.5%</strong>. However, this is calculated on the original loan amount, not the remaining balance. This makes the math surprisingly simple:</p>

      <p style="text-align: center; font-weight: bold; background: #f3f4f6; padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;">
        Total Interest = Loan Amount × 2.5% × Term (Years)
      </p>

      <h3>Example: £50,000 Bounce Back Loan (6-Year Term)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Loan Amount:</strong> £50,000</li>
        <li><strong>Annual Interest:</strong> £50,000 × 0.025 = £1,250 per year.</li>
        <li><strong>Total Interest over 6 years:</strong> £1,250 × 6 = £7,500.</li>
        <li><strong>Total Repayable:</strong> £57,500.</li>
        <li><strong>Monthly Payment (over 72 months):</strong> £57,500 / 72 = <strong>£798.61</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>If you want to see how this affects your broader financial picture alongside other debts, you can use our free <a href="/tools/uk-debt-payoff-calculator">UK Debt Payoff Calculator</a> to see how overpaying this loan could save you money.</p>

      <h2>The Three Pay As You Grow (PAYG) Options</h2>
      <p>If you are struggling to make the standard £798/month payment on a £50k loan, the government introduced Pay As You Grow (PAYG). You have three options to reduce your monthly payments, and you can still request these if you are struggling in 2026:</p>

      <h3>1. Extend the Loan Term to 10 Years</h3>
      <p>You can extend the repayment term from 6 years (72 months) to 10 years (120 months). This lowers your monthly payment significantly, but you will pay more interest overall because the 2.5% is now spread over 10 years.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Monthly Payment on £50k over 10 years:</strong> £50,000 + (£50,000 × 2.5% × 10) = £62,500. £62,500 / 120 = <strong>£520.83/month</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2. Move to Interest-Only Payments</h3>
      <p>You can pause the capital repayment and only pay the interest for 6 months. On a £50k loan, the interest is £1,250 per year, meaning your monthly payment drops to just <strong>£104.16/month</strong> for 6 months. After 6 months, you revert to standard repayments (or extend the term to cover the missed capital).</p>

      <h3>3. Take a Payment Holiday</h3>
      <p>You can request a full payment holiday of up to 6 months. You pay £0 per month during this period. However, the interest still accrues, and the missed payments are added to the end of your loan term.</p>

      <h2>What Happens in 2026 If You Default?</h2>
      <p>Because the government guaranteed 100% of the loan to the bank, the bank cannot aggressively chase you for the money in the same way they would a standard commercial loan. However, <strong>you still owe the debt.</strong></p>
      <p>As the loans mature in 2026 and 2027, lenders are becoming stricter. If you fail to make payments, the lender will try to recover the money through collections. If the debt remains unpaid, it can be passed to a debt collection agency, and in severe cases, it can affect your personal credit file if you signed a personal guarantee.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
      
      <h3>Can I pay off my Bounce Back Loan early in 2026?</h3>
      <p>Yes. There are no early repayment penalties. Because the interest is a flat rate calculated on the original amount, paying it off early saves you money. If you have cash flow available, it is mathematically the best debt to clear first.</p>

      <h3>Does a Bounce Back Loan affect my personal credit score?</h3>
      <p>The BBLS does not show up on your personal credit file as long as you make the payments on time. However, if you default, the lender can register a County Court Judgment (CCJ) against you, which will severely damage your personal credit score.</p>

      <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
      <p>Your Bounce Back Loan is a fixed-cost debt. Because the interest is a flat 2.5%, it is incredibly cheap money compared to credit cards or overdrafts. If you are struggling, contact your lender immediately and request the Pay As You Grow options. Do not ignore it.</p>
      <p>Use our <a href="/tools/uk-debt-payoff-calculator">Debt Payoff Calculator</a> to model your repayments and see your path to being debt-free.</p>
